interop: C and C++ values

A header import (import cstring "<string.h>") makes the header's names usable as
cstring.strlen, cstring.size_t and so on. Calls and values of C/C++ types are written as C++
writes them: the C++ compiler checks the arguments, a C/C++ value keeps C++'s lifetime
rules, and pointers to C/C++ types are raw T* or gp::cptr<T> (a pointer that shares gocpp
ownership), with no nil check. The C built-in types are C.int, C.char, C.size_t..., and
C.cstr(s) makes a C string that lives for the statement (see docs/spec/interop.md).

A C/C++ value in an interface is copied into a box with a descriptor made from its C++ type
(gp::to_iface_foreign); only interfaces without methods can hold one until typed interop
(Phase 12).

Specification: docs/spec/interop.md
See also: gocpp explain generics, gocpp explain checks
Also known as: c, cpp, c++, foreign, cgo

Example (interop.gp):

    package main

    import (
    	"fmt"
    	cstdlib "<stdlib.h>"
    	cstring "<string.h>"
    )

    func main() {
    	n := cstring.strlen(C.cstr("hello")) // a C call with a C string
    	fmt.Println(n, cstdlib.abs(-3))
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/interop.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/interop.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    // ---- ./interop.gp:5: cstdlib "<stdlib.h>"
    #include <stdlib.h>

    // ---- ./interop.gp:6: cstring "<string.h>"
    #include <string.h>

    #include <gocpp/fmtcxx.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./interop.gp:9: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./interop.gp:10: n := cstring.strlen(C.cstr("hello")) // a C call with a C string
    // gp[interop] col 7: a call of a C/C++ function: the C++ compiler checks the arguments, C/C++
    //     values keep C++'s rules (lifetime, no nil checks)
    // gp[interop] col 7: the result of a C/C++ call has a type that gocpp only learns from C++: the
    //     generic lambda [&]<typename gp_R>(std::type_identity<gp_R>) takes it as decltype(call),
    //     outside the lambda, and returns it as gp::values<gp_R> when it is a pair or tuple whose
    //     members must stay alive, or as it is; the call runs once, in one branch
        auto n = [&]<typename gp_R0>(std::type_identity<gp_R0>) { if constexpr (gp::detail::is_values<gp_R0>) { return gp::values<gp_R0>(::strlen(gp::cstr("hello"_gs).gp_ptr())); } else { return (::strlen(gp::cstr("hello"_gs).gp_ptr())); } }(std::type_identity<decltype(::strlen(gp::cstr("hello"_gs).gp_ptr()))>());

    // ---- ./interop.gp:11: fmt.Println(n, cstdlib.abs(-3))
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
    // gp[interop] col 17: a call of a C/C++ function: the C++ compiler checks the arguments, C/C++
    //     values keep C++'s rules (lifetime, no nil checks)
        gp::fmt::Println(gp::cgo::fmt_arg(n), gp::cgo::fmt_arg(::abs(-3)));

    // ---- ./interop.gp:12: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
